---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
---

<Site
  title="AirHound"
  description="AirHound project reference: Wi-Fi and Bluetooth observation, signature matching, desktop and ESP32 setup, supported receivers, and downloads."
>
  <header class="page-header home-header wrap">
    <h1>AirHound</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      AirHound is open-source software for collecting Wi-Fi and Bluetooth
      observations from desktop radios, ESP32 devices, and supported external
      receivers. It records observations and attaches evidence from matching
      signature rules.
    </p>
  </header>
  <div class="wrap content home-content">
    <div class="reference-grid">
      <section aria-labelledby="capabilities">
        <h2 id="capabilities">Capabilities</h2>
        <p>
          Use AirHound to inspect nearby radio activity, collect observations
          during a field session, or investigate signature matches. The
          available data depends on the receiver and operating system.
        </p>
        <div class="capability-list">
          <div>
            <h3>Desktop scanning</h3>
            <p>
              Command-line scanning on macOS and Linux. Wi-Fi and BLE support
              depend on OS permissions and radio configuration.
            </p>
            <a href="/reference/hostd/">Desktop guide</a>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>ESP32 detectors</h3>
            <p>
              Dedicated firmware for M5StickC Plus2 and XIAO ESP32-S3. Board and
              runtime variants have separate validation records.
            </p>
            <a href="/reference/docs/firmware/">Firmware guide</a>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>External receivers</h3>
            <p>
              CatSniffer/Sniffle BLE capture and an experimental ALFA MT7610U
              passive Wi-Fi receiver.
            </p>
            <a href="/hardware/">Receiver support</a>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Signature evidence</h3>
            <p>
              Matching rules, source references, and limitations attached to
              observations. A match does not establish a device’s identity or
              intent.
            </p>
            <a href="/signatures/">Signature catalog</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <aside class="reference-sidebar" aria-labelledby="getting-started">
        <h2 id="getting-started">Getting started</h2>
        <ul class="reference-links">
          <li>
            <a href="/start/#desktop">macOS or Linux</a>
            <p>Installation, permissions, and output</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/start/#firmware">M5StickC Plus2 or XIAO</a>
            <p>Board selection and flashing</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/start/#external">External receivers</a>
            <p>Capture sources and setup</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Project reference</h2>
        <ul class="reference-links compact">
          <li>
            <a href="/reference/docs/">Documentation index</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/downloads/">Downloads</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/reference/docs/release-status/">
              Release and testing status
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://github.com/dougborg/AirHound/issues/160">
              Roadmap
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/contribute/">Contributing</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <section class="reference-section" aria-labelledby="observation-data">
      <h2 id="observation-data">Observation data</h2>
      <p>
        Receiver data enters the shared Rust detection code. Output includes
        ordinary observations as well as signature matches; supported fields and
        export formats vary by source.
      </p>
      <ol class="data-flow" aria-label="Observation processing">
        <li>
          <h3>Radio sources</h3>
          <p>Wi-Fi frames and BLE advertisements</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Signature matching</h3>
          <p>Shared rules and evidence metadata</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Observation output</h3>
          <p>Source data, matches, and limitations</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <a href="/reference/docs/detection-evidence/">
        Detection evidence and output semantics
      </a>
    </section>
    <section
      class="reference-section field-reference"
      aria-labelledby="location-exports"
    >
      <div>
        <h2 id="location-exports">Location and exports</h2>
        <p>
          The desktop scanner can add receiver-location context through gpsd and
          export observations as NDJSON or WiGLE CSV. Missing or stale location
          fixes are not treated as current positions. A receiver’s coordinates
          do not locate a detected device.
        </p>
        <ul class="inline-reference">
          <li>
            <a href="/reference/docs/gnss-wardriving/">
              GPS setup and wardriving notes
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/reference/hostd/#observation-identity-and-export">
              Export formats and identity limits
            </a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <figure class="route-figure">
        <svg
          viewBox="0 0 600 190"
          role="img"
          aria-labelledby="route-title route-description"
        >
          <title id="route-title">Example receiver route</title>
          <desc id="route-description">
            A schematic route with observation markers. The route and markers
            are synthetic and do not show live observations or transmitter
            positions.
          </desc>
          <defs>
            <pattern
              id="route-grid"
              width="60"
              height="46"
              patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
            >
              <path
                d="M0 0H60V46H0Z"
                fill="none"
                stroke="#cbd9eb"
                stroke-width="1"
              />
            </pattern>
          </defs>
          <rect width="600" height="190" fill="url(#route-grid)" />
          <path
            d="M410-10C330 45 485 105 415 200"
            fill="none"
            stroke="#c2d5eb"
            stroke-width="25"
          />
          <path
            d="M0 145L600 32M480-10L210 200"
            fill="none"
            stroke="#f8fafc"
            stroke-width="10"
          />
          <path
            d="M55 143H155V97H275V51H455V97H540"
            fill="none"
            stroke="#1552ce"
            stroke-width="2.5"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
          />
          <g fill="#f2f5f7" stroke="#1552ce" stroke-width="2.5">
            <circle cx="55" cy="143" r="4" />
            <circle cx="155" cy="97" r="6" />
            <circle cx="275" cy="51" r="6" />
            <circle cx="455" cy="97" r="6" />
            <circle cx="540" cy="97" r="4" />
          </g>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Illustrative receiver route. Markers represent observations along the
          route, not transmitter locations.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>
  </div>
</Site>
